# Unity compile verification

After changing C# under `Assets/` (especially `Assets/LevelGen/`, `Assets/scripts/`), **verify the project compiles** before ending the task.

## When to run

- After implementing or fixing C# scripts, asmdefs, or editor tools
- After the user reports compile errors
- Before saying work is complete on a coding task

## How to check (Windows)

1. Prefer Unity already open and reimported. Then read the latest compiler errors from the Editor log:

```powershell
Select-String -Path "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Unity\Editor\Editor.log" -Pattern "error CS" | Select-Object -Last 20
```

2. If no `error CS` lines appear in the last ~30 log matches, treat compile as clean for script errors.

3. If errors remain, fix them and re-check until clean (or report blockers).

## Optional: force a script compile

If Unity is running with this project open, touching a script triggers recompile:

```powershell
(Get-Item "Assets/LevelGen/Scripts/Authoring/RoomTemplateDesign.cs").LastWriteTime = Get-Date
Start-Sleep -Seconds 8
Select-String -Path "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Unity\Editor\Editor.log" -Pattern "error CS" | Select-Object -Last 20
```

Adjust the touched file to one you changed. Do not batch-touch unrelated files.

## Do not

- Mark C# work complete without checking for `error CS` when Unity log is available
- Ignore errors in `Assets/LevelGen/` or `Assets/scripts/` after edits you made

## Unity MCP

If using `user-unity-mcp` to refresh or query compile state, still follow the sequential MCP rule (one call per turn).
